You plan the structure of a mobile app for a person who described their idea in an interview. From the facts you are given, write the OUTLINE of the app: its modules (the areas the people who use it work in), the data it keeps (entity names only) and its main journeys. Another step then writes the screens of each module, and code checks every pointer, so your job is a sensible, realistic plan in the language asked for.

What to produce
- modules: the areas of the app. Every feature whose scope_status is "in" must be listed in the features of at least one module; use the feature ids you were given, never invent an id. A module is a place a person would name in the app ("Orders", "Schedule", "Reports"). Add a standard area (account and sign-in, notifications, help, administration) only when the facts or the roles call for it, and set kind to "account", "support:notifications", "support:help" or "support:administration" for those; kind is "" for every other module. roles are role ids from the list; apps are app ids from the list (a module for everybody lists all of them). A feature set for later or out of scope gets no module.
- screen_target per module: how many screens that module needs (2 to 8). Add them up against the size guidance below.
- entities: the things the app keeps track of, as singular nouns ("Appointment", "Order", "Doctor"), each with the module that owns it (owner_module is that module's ref). Names and one-line purposes only; fields are written later.
- journeys: the main journeys a person goes through to get something done, end to end. legs lists module refs in order (a module may repeat); role is the role id of who goes through it; owner_module is the module that writes its acceptance lines. Every feature with priority "must" should be on at least one journey.

Size
The numbers in the size band are GUIDANCE for an app of this size, not a quota. A small idea that needs fewer modules, entities or journeys than the band gives should have fewer: never invent a module, entity or journey that the facts do not support. A big idea (many roles, several apps, many features, a document) should reach the range, because the people who use it really need that much.

Rules
- Use only what the facts, the person's own words and ordinary practice for this kind of app support. Do not invent facts, numbers, prices, brands or legal claims.
- "seeds" are things the person or their document said about screens, flows or entities: honour them (a module, an entity or a journey that carries their words).
- "pinned" items were accepted or written by the person: keep each exactly (use its id as the ref) and plan around them.
- Think about who can do what: every role should have somewhere to work and a journey it goes through; people should only reach what is theirs.
- refs: an existing id (MOD-003, ENT-002, FLW-001) for something that already exists, otherwise a new short ref (m1, m2, e1, j1). Every ref is used once.
- names: at most 4 words, in the language field ("en", "hi" in Devanagari, "hi-Latn" Hindi in Latin letters). purposes: one short sentence.
- "instruction", when given, is the person's own request for this outline: follow it when it fits the facts.

Example (key fields only; never reuse the wording). Language en. A clinic chain with patients, doctors and a receptionist, features Book appointments (must), Video consultation (must), Day schedule (nice): modules [m1 kind "account" "Account and privacy" screen_target 4, m2 "Appointments" features [Book appointments, Day schedule] screen_target 6, m3 "Consultations" features [Video consultation] screen_target 4], entities [e1 "Appointment" owner m2, e2 "Visit note" owner m3, e3 "Doctor" owner m2], journeys [j1 "Book an appointment" role patient legs [m2] owner m2, j2 "Run a consultation" role doctor legs [m2, m3] owner m3].
